.TH CSTRING 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CSTRING \- overview of the cstring dynamic string
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A cstr is a heap string that grows whenever an operation needs more
space. Its buffer always holds a NUL-terminated C string, so you can
pass the pointer from cstr_c_str(3) straight to any function that takes
a const char *. A cstr holds text, and its content stops at the first
NUL byte.

The capacity of the buffer is always a power of two and never less than
16 bytes. An operation that needs more space grows the buffer to the
smallest power of two that holds the result. cstr_reserve(3) grows it
before you need the space, and cstr_reset(3) shrinks it back to 16
bytes.

There are two layers in the API. The type-inferred macros, such as
cstr_construct(3), cstr_append(3) and cstr_replace(3), stop the program
through _ccol_fatal_err() when an operation fails. The raw functions,
such as cstring_create_full(3), cstring_append(3) and
cstring_replace(3), report a failure in their return value instead.
cstr_copy(3) and cstr_split(3) are the two macros that behave
differently: they call their functions directly and give NULL on
failure.

Every argument that gives text to add is a plain C string. The calls
that change a string (cstr_append(3), cstr_prepend(3), cstr_insert(3),
cstr_set(3) and their cstring_ functions) accept a str argument that
points into the buffer of that same string, as in
cstr_append(s, cstr_c_str(s)), and they read str correctly even when
they reallocate the buffer.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
The pointer from cstr_c_str(3) points into the buffer of the string.
That buffer belongs to the string and you never free it. Any operation
that changes the string can move the buffer, so the pointer is valid
only until the next such operation.

cstr_substring(3), cstr_copy(3) and cstr_split(3) give new strings that
you own and must destroy. cstr_split(3) gives a cvec of cstr, so you
destroy each element first and then the vector.
.fi
.SH "USE WITH THE MAPS"
.nf
chashmap and cbstmap do not accept cstr as a key type or a value type;
they accept char *, and they hash and compare such a key by its content.
Give the result of cstr_c_str(3), cast to char *, as the key or the
value. The map copies the characters into its own storage during the
insert, so after the insert you can change or destroy the cstr without
any effect on the map.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A string has no internal lock. You can share one string between threads,
but then you must serialize every call that changes the string against
all the other calls on that string.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

int main(void) {
    cstr_construct(path, "/var/log");
    cstr_append(path, "/");
    cstr_append(path, "app.log");
    printf("%s (%zu)\en", cstr_c_str(path), cstr_length(path));
    cstr_destroy(path);
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
